      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, structuring audits and monitoring in a collaborative way can help maintain a positive relationship while mitigating risks. Here are a few strategies:\n\n1. Regular Scheduled Reviews – Plan routine meetings to check-in on supply, deliver quality improvement plans if gaps are found.\n2. Surprise Spot Checks – Random, unscheduled reviews to keep the supplier vigilant. Frame these as opportunities to improve your own processes.\n3. Joint Audits – Where feasible, involve the supplier in the audit process to foster transparency.\n4. Risk-Based Auditing – Focus on areas with the highest risk to production or cost, rather than a full-scope audit every time.\n5. Performance Metrics – Track key performance indicators, and openly discuss where the supplier is excelling or needing improvement.\n6. Quality Assurance Field Checks – Have 2 staff from your end visit supplier facilities during production to assess operations firsthand.\n\nBy approaching audits and monitoring this way, they can act as built-in checks that benefit both parties by ensuring high performance and quality, shifting the perspective from purely scrutinizing activities to enhancing overall process quality.",
